337X393 2022-04-04 Robotic_27,337X393 2022-04-04 Robotic,75,337X393,null,"Part Condition: Good Part Description: All compressor stator vanes were cleaned and visually inspected. Per the FMI, S0s were replaced with enhanced high output S0s while the S5s were replaced with new robust S5 vanes. Overall, stator vanes were in good condition and only two blades required PAC disposition for blend repairs: • S8-V44 • S10-V40 Bucket techs recorded all stator vane drop checks and fixed as needed. Shims were added on stages 6,7,8,11, and 12 in order to correct drops. Stage 17 vanes are shrouded and found to have two segments with the welded bolt configuration (older style). This original shrouded S17 configuration (introduced in 2004) consisted of a bushing, washer, and bolt, where the bolt was tack welded to the top face of the bushing to serve as a secondary bolt locking feature. Several units with this shrouded S17 configuration have been found with damage to the bolts, shrouds, and/or bushings. The damage is characterized by the bolt losing pre-load, causing the bushing to become detached and causing vane wear. In 2008, shrouded S17 configuration featuring a staked bolt (Figure 4) as opposed to a welded bolt, was introduced to help prevent the potential loss of bolt pre-load due to welding. In addition, the washer was eliminated due to the increased size of the bushing. In 2016, a new shrouded S17 configuration with change in bushing material to cobalt-chromium based alloy having an improved wear couple and being used within GE Aviation in similar applications was introduced. A PAC case was submitted, ER-20220415-0544, on these segments to determine if these can be installed back in the unit. As per the close notes, ""the older style S17's seem to be in good condition and the welds are holding and bushings are all properly aligned. There's no evidence of rocking in the casing so these parts can return to service."" Recommendation Status: Should be planned for next Outage R