337X393 2022-04-04 Robotic_78,337X393 2022-04-04 Robotic,284,337X393,null,"Part Description: Explanation of DC Leakage Tests The direct current leakage test can be considered a series of high voltage insulation resistance measurements taken at increasing steps of test voltage on a scheduled time basis (R = E/I). The time schedule is based on a preliminary dielectric absorption test to determine the absorption ratio of the winding insulation. The absorption ratio is then used to determine the time schedule for the step-voltage test (Scheif Method). The test results obtained using this method are the same as would be obtained by applying each step- voltage level to the winding for 10 minutes (as individual tests). Thus, the test results are essentially available leakage currents plus a constant absorption current at each test voltage. The D.C. leakage test with a maximum test value of 2E is normally considered a non- destructive test, however, failures can occur on a marginal winding. This test is accomplished by applying a DC voltage in steps up to a previously determined limit or until abnormalities are found which dictate that the test should be terminated. The applied voltage at each step is plotted against the measured leakage current at that Voltage, to form a leakage current curve. DC Leakage testing was performed to see if there was any indication of the unit showing signs of insulation breakdown. Test was initiated for 10 minutes at 15Kv. The test was then stepped up 3Kv at calculated time intervals up to the customer agreed voltage of 36Kv. The test had a passing result which indicated an acceptable insulation system of the winding (please see attached data). g Armature Insulation DC Leakage Test Date(m/d/y) 4/8/2022 Generator Serial No. 337X393 Prepared by C.Cardwell Customer NVEnergy Turbine Serial No. M.Blanchard Manufacture GE FSR No. I01-069675 A 15 KV DC voltage was applied to each phase with the other two phases grounded for a period of 10 minutes. Readings were recorded at specific time intervals. On the basis of these readings a time schedule is established for reading stabilized leakage currents at successively higher voltage values up to a maximum value, approximately twice line-to-line voltage. The current readings are plotted as a function of voltage to determine if a nearly linear relationship exists, indicating a constant insulation resistance as a function of voltage. Time Left: Center: Right: Winding (Minutes) mA Megohm mA Megohm mA Megohm Temp (ºC) 0.50 4.60 3300 3.50 4300 3.50 4300 17.8 1.00 3.10 4800 3.60 4200 2.90 5200 1.50 2.30 6500 2.10 7100 2.10 7100 Wet Bulb (ºF) 2.00 1.80 8300 1.90 7900 1.10 14000 5.4 3.00 1.30 12000 1.50 10000 0.80 19000 3.16 1.00 15000 1.10 14000 1.50 10000 Dry Bulb (ºF) 4.00 1.10 14000 0.70 21000 1.00 15000 5.00 0.90 17000 0.80 19000 0.80 19000 6.00 0.90 17000 1.00 15000 1.10 14000 Relative 7.00 0.80 19000 1.20 13000 0.70 21000 Humidity 8.00 0.70 21000 1.20 13000 0.90 17000 25.78 9.00 0.70 21000 1.30 12000 0.70 21000 10.00 0.70 21000 0.90 17000 0.60 25000 PI 4.43 4.00 4.83 N 49 157 3 % of Left: Center: Right: 15 KV Time KV mA Time KV mA Time KV mA 100 10' 15 0.70 10' 15 0.90 10' 15 0.60 120 14'25"" 18 1.00 14'25"" 18 1.00 13'27"" 18 0.80 140 18'17"" 21 1.30 18'17"" 21 1.20 16'21"" 21 1.00 160 21'48"" 24 1.30 21'48"" 24 1.70 18'55"" 24 1.10 180 25'3"" 27 1.80 25'3"" 27 2.00 21'12"" 27 1.50 200 28'5"" 30 2.10 28'5"" 30 2.50 23'18"" 30 2.20 220 30'57"" 33 2.20 30'57"" 33 2.80 25'14"" 33 2.70 240 33'39"" 36 2.70 33'39"" 36 3.20 27'1"" 36 3.00 260 36'13"" 39 36'13"" 39 28'41"" 39 280 38'41"" 42 38'41"" 42 30'15"" 42 300 41'1"" 45 41'1"" 45 31'44"" 45 320 43'17"" 48 43'17"" 48 33'8"" 48 340 45'27"" 51 45'27"" 51 34'27"" 51 360 47'32"" 54 47'32"" 54 35'43"" 54 380 49'33"" 57 49'33"" 57 36'55"" 57 400 51'30"" 60 51'30"" 60 38'4"" 60 420 53'24"" 63 53'24"" 63 39'10"" 63 HIPOT HIPOT HIPOT D3162044 Sheet: (a) Rev 1.1 - 03/28/13 GE Energy Proprietary Information Armature Insulation DC 