337X393 2022-04-04 Robotic_81,337X393 2022-04-04 Robotic,291,337X393,null,"Part Condition: Good Part Description: Background General Electric wedge tightness requires the use of the Proceq Equotip Model SVP-40 Hardness Tester. The Equotip principle of hardness testing is based on a spring-loaded impact body that is propelled against a test piece. The forward velocity is measured and compared to the reverse velocity (after rebound). The relationship between these two velocities is related to hardness and is assigned a dimensionless number referred to as ""Leebs"". Wedge tightness values are categorized based on three ranges of tightness. These categories are loose, hollow, and tight. This test is performed to accurately quantify stator wedge tightness and is used to assess the need for wedge tightening or replacement. A “tight” stator winding can last two or three times as long as a winding that is not firmly held in the stator core. Test results are used to generate a “wedge tightness map,” which clearly shows the wedge tightness within the generator. A thorough visual inspection and wedge tightness checks should be performed at every major outage where the generator field is removed from the stator or with a generator In-Situ Inspection which was the method used for this inspection. General Electric performed a MAGIC inspection to determine wedge tightness while the field was still in place with minimal disassembly to the generator stator. For additional details refer to GE publication (GEK 103566) entitled ""Creating an Effective Maintenance Program"". As part of the wedge tightness test, wedge tapping was performed on each wedge. During the tightness only one (1) wedge fell below the ""tight"" category which is indicated on the wedge tightness report or more commonly referred to as ""Wedge Map"". Test results were passing",2026-04-13T02:36:43.024Z,2026-04-13T02:36:43.024Z,"[-0.008147644810378551,-0.0026414073072373867,-0.010615742765367031,-0.009262674488127232,0